# ============================================================
# PROLOGUE — "OVERBURDEN"
# 2026. The dig at Dholarath, first light. ~20 minutes.
# ============================================================
#
# DRAFT BY CLAUDE — written from docs/kaalchakra-story-bible.md §5 so you have
# something to argue with instead of a blank page. Every line is yours to cut.
# Places I made a call you might not agree with are marked  # ?? DECISION
#
# It teaches movement, climbing and excavation without ever saying the word
# "tutorial", and ends on the hard cut to 1900 BCE.
#
# ---- how to write in this file -----------------------------------------
#   #     a note to yourself or me. Ignored by the game.
#   ::    starts a chunk the game can jump to
#   NAME: who is speaking
#   (..)  stage direction — for the actor, and for me
#   >     a choice the player taps
#   ->    jump to another chunk
#   @     an instruction to me
#
# If you want something that isn't in that list — a camera move, a sound, her
# hands shaking — just write it. The format bends to the story, not the other
# way round. I'll make whatever you write work.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------


:: start
@ era present
@ time_of_day first_light
@ music bansuri_sparse
# She is filming on her phone, walking the top of the trench. Player has
# control from the first word — no opening cutscene.
@ objective "Walk the trench line"
EVA: (to the phone, quiet, the whole site still asleep) Day nineteen.
EVA: Sun's not up. Ishita's not up. The monsoon is three weeks early and
EVA: nobody at the Institute wants to talk about what that does to a trench.
EVA: (beat) But you lot didn't come here for drainage.
EVA: (turning the camera to the pit) You came for this.
-> the_pitch


:: the_pitch
EVA: Dholarath. Four and a half thousand years old. Grid streets, covered
EVA: drains, a reservoir that could hold a season of rain —
EVA: — and a writing system that nobody on Earth can read.
EVA: Four hundred signs. Thousands of inscriptions. Not one of them understood.
# ?? DECISION — this is where she names her mother's theory. I put it in the
# cold open because it is the engine of the whole game and the player should
# hear it before anything supernatural happens. You may want it later.
EVA: Thirty years ago somebody worked out why. She said: stop reading it
EVA: like a language. It isn't sentences. It's an index — labels, pointing
EVA: at something that isn't the writing.
EVA: The field laughed her out of the room.
EVA: (flat) She's my mother. So, you know. Personal stake.
@ objective "Get down into Trench 4"
-> ishita_arrives


:: ishita_arrives
# Teaching the climb down. Ishita arrives at the lip above her.
ISHITA: (above, dry) Two million people and you still can't operate a kettle.
EVA: Two million people fund your remote sensing, Dr Vaid.
ISHITA: Two million people think you found a "lost city." You found a
ISHITA: well-documented site with a new trench in it.
EVA: Two million people have hope. You should try it.
> "Come and look at the floor."        -> ishita_down      @ flag ishita_invited
> "Go back to bed."                    -> ishita_stays
> (say nothing — climb down)           -> ishita_stays


:: ishita_down
@ flag ishita_present_at_discovery
ISHITA: (sliding down, landing badly) ...What am I looking at?
EVA: That's what I'm asking you. You're the one with the ground scans.
-> teach_dig


:: ishita_stays
ISHITA: (fading, above) When you find Atlantis, wake me.
EVA: (to herself) Rude.
-> teach_dig


:: teach_dig
# EXCAVATION TAUGHT HERE — first on something worthless, so failing costs
# nothing and the player learns the verb before it matters.
@ skill_unlock excavation
@ objective "Clear the trench floor"
EVA: (to the phone, settling in) Right. Slowly. We don't take, we record.
EVA: (a beat, to herself, almost ritual) Brush, don't scrape. Dad's rule.
# ?? DECISION — her father's trowel. The bible says she carries it; I wanted
# it established before the chamber, not after, so the discovery isn't the
# first time the player hears about him. It's one line. Cut it if it's too early.
-> first_find


:: first_find
@ sfx trowel_on_pottery
EVA: (genuine delight) Oh, hello.
EVA: (holding it up to the lens) Potsherd. Red slip. Probably a storage jar,
EVA: probably nothing, probably the single most beautiful nothing you'll
EVA: see today.
@ phone_available sakshi
@ objective "Keep clearing — something is under the silt"
-> trench_work


:: trench_work
# The optional Sakshi call. Strongly cued, never forced — this is the first
# time the player meets the hint system, so it has to feel like a choice.
> Call Ma                              -> call_sakshi
> Keep working                         -> the_lintel


:: call_sakshi
@ sfx phone_dial
@ music sakshi_theme
SAKSHI: (crackly line, instantly awake) You're in the trench.
EVA: How do you always —
SAKSHI: Because it's five in the morning and you're whispering.
EVA: (caught) Ma.
SAKSHI: What did you find?
EVA: Red slip sherd. And the floor's wrong. It's too flat, and it's too
EVA: flat in a straight line.
SAKSHI: (a long pause) Check the drainage, beta. Not the walls. Everyone
SAKSHI: always checks the walls.
SAKSHI: I told the reviewers that in '98 and they laughed at me for an hour.
# ?? DECISION — "beta" and "Ma". Your call on the register: Ma / Mumma / Amma,
# and how much Gujarati or Hindi threads through. I defaulted to the lightest
# touch. This is exactly the sort of thing I should not be deciding.
EVA: (quiet) I know, Ma.
SAKSHI: Don't "I know" me. Go and look at the drain.
@ flag called_sakshi_prologue
@ objective "Follow the drain line"
-> the_lintel


:: the_lintel
# The find. Camera pushes in; she stops performing for the camera here, which
# is the tell that this one is real.
@ camera push_in sealed_door
@ music drop_out
@ sfx brush_on_stone
EVA: (to the phone, still presenting) Okay, so this is — this is probably
EVA: a kerb stone, these run along the —
EVA: (stops)
EVA: (long pause)
EVA: That's a lintel.
-> clear_the_door


:: clear_the_door
# Second excavation, now it matters. Bigger, slower, timed? -- see note.
# ?? DECISION — I did NOT put a fail state here. First real discovery of the
# game should not be something you can botch. Failure comes in Chapter 2.
@ objective "Clear the doorway"
@ music low_drone_build
ISHITA: [if ishita_present_at_discovery] (very quietly) Eva. That's a door.
EVA: [if ishita_present_at_discovery] I know it's a door.
ISHITA: [if ishita_present_at_discovery] That's a *sealed* door.
EVA: (not to the phone any more) Ma was right.
-> the_chamber


:: the_chamber
@ sfx stone_grinding
@ camera slow_reveal chamber
@ music silence
# Hold on silence. No score. Let the room land.
EVA: (barely audible) ...Oh.
# The skeleton, seated upright, hands cupped. The wall of signs behind it.
EVA: (to nobody) There's someone in here.
EVA: Sitting up. Not buried — *placed*. Facing the door.
EVA: And the whole back wall is covered in signs.
@ objective "Read the wall"
-> the_wall


:: the_wall
EVA: I can't read any of it. Nobody can read any of it.
EVA: (moving closer) Except —
EVA: (stopping) Except that pairing. There, and there, and — six times.
EVA: That's the pairing off the north gate signboard.
EVA: The one every tourist photograph in Gujarat has in the background.
EVA: (the thought arriving) It's a label. It's labelling something.
EVA: It's an *index*.
@ flag saw_the_index
-> the_wheel


:: the_wheel
@ camera push_in wheel
EVA: (down to the skeleton's hands) And you're holding something.
EVA: Copper. Disc. Eight spokes.
EVA: (the archaeologist and the daughter arguing in one sentence)
EVA: I should not touch this. I should photograph this, and log this, and
EVA: call it in, and not touch this.
> Lift it                              -> lift
> Photograph it first                  -> photograph


:: photograph
@ sfx phone_shutter
@ flag recorded_before_taking
# ?? DECISION — rewarding her creed with a real flag. "We don't take, we
# record" should mean something mechanically, even here. I'd like this to pay
# off in Chapter 5 when HELIX argues she's no different from them.
EVA: (steadier) Recorded. Logged. Witnessed.
EVA: Okay.
-> lift


:: lift
@ sfx low_hum_begin
EVA: (lifting it) It's warm.
EVA: (confused, quieter) Why is it warm —
@ hard_cut
-> arrival


:: arrival
# THE HARD CUT. No transition, no fade. One frame 2026, next frame 1900 BCE.
# Trench walls brick-new. Water thundering somewhere above. Dust and noise and
# a city that is extremely alive.
@ era past
@ sfx water_roar
@ music dholarath_alive
@ camera whip_around
EVA: (no breath in her) What.
VASUDHA: (unimpressed, holding a plumb line) Late.
VASUDHA: You Keepers are always late.
@ title_card "KAALCHAKRA"
-> END
